      "text": "To find the number of integers \\( n \\) that satisfy the inequality \\( 10 < n^2 < 99 \\), we need to determine the range of \\( n \\) values that satisfy this condition.\n\nFirst, let's break down the inequality into two parts:\n1. \\( n^2 > 10 \\)\n2. \\( n^2 < 99 \\)\n\nWe will solve each part separately and then find the intersection of the solutions.\n\n### Solving \\( n^2 > 10 \\)\n\nWe need to find the values of \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 > 10 \\). This can be rewritten as:\n\\[ n > \\sqrt{10} \\]\nSince \\( \\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16 \\), the smallest integer greater than 3.16 is 4.\n\nSo, \\( n \\) must be at least 4.\n\n### Solving \\( n^2 < 99 \\)\n\nWe need to find the values of \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 < 99 \\). This can be rewritten as:\n\\[ n < \\sqrt{99} \\]\nSince \\( \\sqrt{99} \\approx 9.95 \\), the largest integer less than 9.95 is 9.\n\nSo, \\( n \\) must be at most 9.\n\n### Combining the Solutions\n\nCombining the results from both inequalities, we get:\n\\[ 4 \\leq n \\leq 9 \\]\n\nNow, we need to count the integers from 4 to 9 inclusive.\n\nThe integers in this range are:\n\\[ 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 \\]\n\nCounting these integers, we get:\n\\[ 9 - 4 + 1 = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the number of integers \\( n \\) that satisfy \\( 10 < n^2 < 99 \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\nTo verify, let's list all the integers from 4 to 9 and check if their squares fall within the given range:\n- \\( 4^2 = 16 \\) (valid)\n- \\( 5^2 = 25 \\) (valid)\n- \\( 6^2 = 36 \\) (valid)\n- \\( 7^2 = 49 \\) (valid)\n- \\( 8^2 = 64 \\) (valid)\n- \\( 9^2 = 81 \\) (valid)\n\nAll these squares are indeed between 10 and 99. Therefore, the solution is correct.",
